NNAAMMEE
     hhaassttdd — Highly Available Storage daemon

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     hhaassttdd [--ddFFhh] [--cc _c_o_n_f_i_g] [--PP _p_i_d_f_i_l_e]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The hhaassttdd daemon is responsible for managing highly available GEOM
     providers.

     hhaassttdd allows the transparent storage of data on two physically separated
     machines connected over a TCP/IP network.  Only one machine (cluster
     node) can actively use storage provided by hhaassttdd.  This machine is called
     primary.  The hhaassttdd daemon operates on block level, which makes it trans‐
     parent to file systems and applications.

     There is one main hhaassttdd daemon which starts new worker process as soon as
     a role for the given resource is changed to primary or as soon as a role
     for the given resource is changed to secondary and remote (primary) node
     will successfully connect to it.  Every worker process gets a new process
     title (see setproctitle(3)), which describes its role and resource it
     controls.  The exact format is:

           hastd: <resource name> (<role>)

     If (and only if) hhaassttdd operates in primary role for the given resource, a
     corresponding _/_d_e_v_/_h_a_s_t_/_<_n_a_m_e_> disk-like device (GEOM provider) is cre‐
     ated.  File systems and applications can use this provider to send I/O
     requests to.  Every write, delete and flush operation (BIO_WRITE,
     BIO_DELETE, BIO_FLUSH) is sent to the local component and replicated on
     the remote (secondary) node if it is available.  Read operations
     (BIO_READ) are handled locally unless an I/O error occurs or the local
     version of the data is not up-to-date yet (synchronization is in
     progress).

     The hhaassttdd daemon uses the GEOM Gate class to receive I/O requests from
     the in-kernel GEOM infrastructure.  The ggeeoomm__ggaattee..kkoo module is loaded au‐
     tomatically if the kernel was not compiled with the following option:

           ooppttiioonnss GGEEOOMM__GGAATTEE

     The connection between two hhaassttdd daemons is always initiated from the one
     running as primary to the one running as secondary.  When the primary
     hhaassttdd is unable to connect or the connection fails, it will try to re-es‐
     tablish the connection every few seconds.  Once the connection is estab‐
     lished, the primary hhaassttdd will synchronize every extent that was modified
     during connection outage to the secondary hhaassttdd.

     It is possible that in the case of a connection outage between the nodes
     the hhaassttdd primary role for the given resource will be configured on both
     nodes.  This in turn leads to incompatible data modifications.  Such a
     condition is called a split-brain and cannot be automatically resolved by
     the hhaassttdd daemon as this will lead most likely to data corruption or loss
     of important changes.  Even though it cannot be fixed by hhaassttdd itself, it
     will be detected and a further connection between independently modified
     nodes will not be possible.  Once this situation is manually resolved by
     an administrator, the resource on one of the nodes can be initialized
     (erasing local data), which makes a connection to the remote node possi‐
     ble again.  Connection of the freshly initialized component will trigger
     full resource synchronization.

     A hhaassttdd daemon never picks its role automatically.  The role has to be
     configured with the hastctl(8) control utility by additional software
     like uuccaarrpp or hheeaarrttbbeeaatt that can reliably manage role separation and
     switch secondary node to primary role in case of the primary's failure.

     The hhaassttdd daemon can be started with the following command line argu‐
     ments:

     --cc _c_o_n_f_i_g   Specify alternative location of the configuration file.  The
                 default location is _/_e_t_c_/_h_a_s_t_._c_o_n_f.

     --dd          Print or log debugging information.  This option can be spec‐
                 ified multiple times to raise the verbosity level.

     --FF          Start the hhaassttdd daemon in the foreground.  By default hhaassttdd
                 starts in the background.

     --hh          Print the hhaassttdd usage message.

     --PP _p_i_d_f_i_l_e  Specify alternative location of a file where main process PID
                 will be stored.  The default location is _/_v_a_r_/_r_u_n_/_h_a_s_t_d_._p_i_d.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/hast.conf      The configuration file for hhaassttdd and hastctl(8).
     /var/run/hastctl    Control socket used by the hastctl(8) control utility
                         to communicate with hhaassttdd.
     /var/run/hastd.pid  The default location of the hhaassttdd PID file.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     Exit status is 0 on success, or one of the values described in
     sysexits(3) on failure.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Launch hhaassttdd on both nodes.  Set role for resource sshhaarreedd to primary on
     nnooddeeAA and to secondary on nnooddeeBB.  Create file system on _/_d_e_v_/_h_a_s_t_/_s_h_a_r_e_d
